# tools/r/remember_git_nib.rish -- derive the operator card's Git nib from git, and write it on request.
#
# WHY. `.claude/rules/remember-git-nib.md` asks a hand to copy ten hexadecimal characters into
# `construction/ITINERARY.md` at the end of every send. A round on `20260906.010726` measured what
# that costs across a fleet: the nib was falsified four times in ninety minutes -- three by peers
# landing while the round packaged, and once by a hand typing `f1498bd7f5` where git answers
# `f1498bd7fc`, one wrong character in the last position, inside the very note arguing for this
# program. A hand copying hex under time pressure has a per-character failure rate. `git rev-parse`
# has none. The spec is `active-designing/date/20260906/20260906-010726_the-numbers-a-hand-types-that-a-rebase-falsifies.md`.
#
# WHICH COMMIT, and why the caller names it. A commit cannot contain its own hash, so the nib is
# always written in the same breath as the commit it names -- and the rule describes TWO such
# breaths, which this program implemented as one for nine days (REDS %803).
#
#   amend      rule 2. The send pins, then amends after the final rebase. HEAD is about to be
#              rewritten, so the nib names `HEAD^` -- a commit the round was built on, which every
#              clone already holds. Writing HEAD here names the PRE-AMEND sibling, an object no
#              other clone has, and that stood as a double-red on five bodies out of six (%401).
#   follow-up  rule 5. A commit lands ON TOP of the work commit -- a session log recording facts
#              that did not exist until the send was over. HEAD is not about to move; it is about
#              to become the new commit's PARENT, so the nib names `HEAD`.
#   lead-in    rule 5 again, one end of the lap over: the claim commit the ORDER clause asks for
#              BEFORE the build. Its arithmetic is identical -- HEAD becomes its parent -- so this
#              is a PEER NAME for `follow-up` under `.claude/rules/alias-sameness.md` rather than a
#              third shape: one implementation, one refusal path, two lawful words. A lap opening a
#              claim was otherwise asked to call a lead-in a follow-up, which is a word the tool
#              made it get wrong. `tools/f/fleet_claim.sh` now carries the card itself, so this
#              name is for a hand committing a claim by other means.
#
# The two differ by exactly one commit, and running the amend shape where rule 5 asks writes the
# grandparent: a hash that resolves, is an ancestor of HEAD, and is none of the three states
# `remember_git_nib_witness.rish` accepts. So the guard reds on the next lap, naming a round that
# already passed.
#
# WHY IT REFUSES TO GUESS. Nothing this program can read tells a lap about to amend from a lap about
# to commit afresh: both stand at the same HEAD, with the same index, in the same tree. The
# difference lives entirely in what the hand does NEXT. A writer that picks for you is right half
# the time and silent about it, which is worse than the hand it replaced -- a tool's answer carries
# no doubt. So `write` names its shape or it refuses, and a bare render prints BOTH candidates with
# the rule each serves rather than one answer a hand will copy.
#
# WHEN TO RUN IT. At the END of the send, after the final rebase and before the commit that carries
# the card. Every number in this class is correct when written and falsified by an event the writer
# cannot see, so writing it at packaging time is writing it too early.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT A FLAG ON THE GUARD, which is what the spec proposed. `remember_git_nib_witness.rish`
# says twice in its own header that it never edits the card, and a guard that repairs what it
# measures can no longer testify about it: a fault in the writer would be written, and then reported
# green by the same hand. So the two stand free -- this writes, that judges, and neither imports the
# other (`foundations/20260823-204456_single-stranded.md`). The precedent is already in the tree:
# `readme_metrics.rish` writes and `readme_metrics_witness.rish` judges.
#
# USAGE
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/remember_git_nib.rish                    # both shapes, touch nothing
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/remember_git_nib.rish amend              # the rule 2 nib alone
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/remember_git_nib.rish follow-up          # the rule 5 nib alone
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/remember_git_nib.rish write amend        # write the rule 2 nib
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/remember_git_nib.rish write follow-up    # write the rule 5 nib
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/remember_git_nib.rish write lead-in      # the same nib, its other name
#
# `write` with no shape REFUSES and touches nothing. Idempotent: a second write reports
# `card_changed=no` and does not open the file. Proven by
# tools/r/remember_git_nib_write_witness.rish. Run from the repository root.

# The grammar: a verb, and a shape. A shape word standing alone renders that shape.
let argc_ok = "no"
if length args == 0 then let argc_ok = "yes"
if length args == 1 then let argc_ok = "yes"
if length args == 2 then let argc_ok = "yes"
assert argc_ok == "yes" else "remember-git-nib: at most two words -- a verb (render|write) and a shape (amend|follow-up|lead-in)"

let verb = "render"
if length args != 0 then let verb = args[0]
let shape = ""
if length args == 2 then let shape = args[1]

if verb == "amend" then let shape = "amend"
if verb == "follow-up" then let shape = "follow-up"
if verb == "lead-in" then let shape = "lead-in"
if verb == "amend" then let verb = "render"
if verb == "follow-up" then let verb = "render"
if verb == "lead-in" then let verb = "render"

# invariant: the peer name resolves to one shape before anything reads it, so `lead-in` and
# `follow-up` share one derivation, one refusal, and one printed line -- alias-sameness, where
# several lawful names carry one referent and none outranks another.
if shape == "lead-in" then let shape = "follow-up"

# invariant: an unknown word refuses rather than falling through to a default, because the default
# IS the fault this program was repaired for -- a shape chosen by the tool rather than by the hand.
let verb_ok = "no"
if verb == "render" then let verb_ok = "yes"
if verb == "write" then let verb_ok = "yes"
assert verb_ok == "yes" else "remember-git-nib: unknown word ${verb} -- say render, write, amend, follow-up, or lead-in"

let shape_named = "no"
if shape == "amend" then let shape_named = "yes"
if shape == "follow-up" then let shape_named = "yes"
let shape_ok = shape_named
if shape == "" then let shape_ok = "yes"
assert shape_ok == "yes" else "remember-git-nib: unknown shape ${shape} -- say amend (rule 2, HEAD^), or follow-up / lead-in (rule 5, HEAD)"

# invariant: a WRITE names its shape. This is the whole repair of REDS %803 -- nothing readable here
# tells a lap about to amend from one about to commit afresh, so the program asks rather than picks.
let write_ok = "yes"
if verb == "write" then let write_ok = shape_named
assert write_ok == "yes" else "remember-git-nib: write REFUSES to guess which shape you want -- say `write amend` for rule 2 (the amend after the final rebase, HEAD^) or `write follow-up` for rule 5 (a commit landing on top of the work commit, HEAD). The card is untouched."

let card = "construction/ITINERARY.md"
let have = run ["test" "-f" card]
assert have.ok else "remember-git-nib: construction/ITINERARY.md not found -- run from the repository root"

# Both nibs are derived, never recited. `--verify --quiet` makes a root commit answer empty here
# rather than producing a string that would later be written as a nib.
let head_probe = run ["sh" "-c" "git rev-parse --short=10 HEAD"]
assert head_probe.ok else "remember-git-nib: git named no HEAD -- this writes nothing into a repository with no commits"
let head_nib = trim head_probe.out
assert head_nib != "" else "remember-git-nib: git named no HEAD commit"

let parent_probe = run ["sh" "-c" "git rev-parse --verify --quiet HEAD^ >/dev/null 2>&1 && git rev-parse --short=10 HEAD^ || true"]
let parent_nib = trim parent_probe.out

# invariant: a root commit has no parent, so the amend shape names no nib there -- and the follow-up
# shape still does. The refusal follows the shape that was asked for.
let parent_ok = "yes"
if parent_nib == "" then let parent_ok = "no"
if shape == "follow-up" then let parent_ok = "yes"
assert parent_ok == "yes" else "remember-git-nib: HEAD has no parent -- the amend shape names no nib on a root commit, and this writes nothing"

let nib = parent_nib
if shape == "follow-up" then let nib = head_nib

# invariant: it resolves to a commit HERE before it is written ANYWHERE. This is the exact check
# that caught the spec's fourth firing, where the card named an object the repository did not hold.
let kind = run ["sh" "-c" "git cat-file -t ${nib} 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' \n'"]
assert (trim kind.out) == "commit" else "remember-git-nib: the derived nib does not name a commit in this repository -- ${nib}"

# Render names the rule beside the hash, so a hand reading the line learns which move it is making.
let show_amend = "no"
if shape == "" then let show_amend = "yes"
if shape == "amend" then let show_amend = "yes"
let show_follow = "no"
if shape == "" then let show_follow = "yes"
if shape == "follow-up" then let show_follow = "yes"
if verb == "write" then let show_amend = "no"
if verb == "write" then let show_follow = "no"

if show_amend == "yes" then say "remember-git-nib: rule 2, the amend after the final rebase -- HEAD's parent is ${parent_nib} -- write it with: rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/remember_git_nib.rish write amend"
if show_follow == "yes" then say "remember-git-nib: rule 5, a follow-up commit landing on top of the work commit -- HEAD is ${head_nib} -- write it with: rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/remember_git_nib.rish write follow-up"
if verb == "render" then exit exit-ok

# The bytes move in the fixture, the claim stays here: the language that checks the fact owns the
# fact, and the tool that moves bytes moves bytes (readme_metrics_splice.sh's own division).
let wrote = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/r/remember_git_nib_write.sh" card nib]
assert wrote.ok else "remember-git-nib: the write REFUSED -- see stderr; the card is untouched"
assert wrote.out contains "nib_written=${nib}" else "remember-git-nib: the write must name the nib it wrote"
say wrote.out

# invariant: the card reads back through the GUARD's own extractor as the nib just derived, so this
# program never reports success over a card the guard would still refuse.
let back = run ["sh" "-c" "awk '/Git nib:/{ if (match($0, /[0-9a-f]{7,40}/)) { print substr($0, RSTART, RLENGTH); exit } }' construction/ITINERARY.md"]
assert back.ok else "remember-git-nib: the card could not be read back after the write"
assert (trim back.out) == nib else "remember-git-nib: the card reads back as ${back.out_brief} rather than ${nib} -- the guard would still refuse"

say "remember-git-nib: the card names ${nib} -- the ${shape} shape, resolvable in every clone."
